branch: feature/optics-outside-routes-query
worktree: ../okay-wt-query
spec: specs/optics-outside.md (stage 3)

why: stage 1 gave a path three interpreters, and the DESCRIBE one has
  no consumer yet that renders anything — which is the "no production
  caller" trap this arc already names twice. Before a renderer is
  worth writing, a route has to be able to describe the rest of a
  request: a query string. And query parameters are not filler, they
  are where the prism law gets interesting — they are UNORDERED and
  OPTIONAL, so `unapply(url(a)) == Some(a)` forces a decision about
  ordering and about absent-versus-empty that the path never had to
  make.

what: `Route` gains query parameters, keeping the law and the three
  interpreters. `describe` grows the query half, which is what the
  OpenAPI/MCP renderer (the lane after this) needs as input.

also (folded in, both textual, one gate): `Route.Concat` is renamed
  `Route.Split`. The operator read the name and took it for
  `scala.Tuple.Concat`, which is a fair reading and the reason to
  change it — the typeclass's whole addition over the stdlib match
  type IS `split`. And the nested-pairs alternative (`Route[(A, B)]`
  instead of a flat tuple) goes into Decisions as considered and
  rejected, with the reason: the flattening has to happen somewhere,
  and pairs move it from the combinator to every consumer.

next: rename + Decisions commit, then the query design
